d to do a lot of  work at the wall but I had all the  king's landing stuff pretty much as  you've seen it the Cersei stuff to Jaime  stuff all of that so at that point I was  wrestling well with for one thing the  fans and my publishers both were howling  for a book and it already been it  already been five years and I'd missed  several deadlines I kept promising when  I was going to deliver the book you know  I'm I'd originally in a crazed excessive  optimism had to hope to deliver Dance  with Dragons in 2002 so it could be  published in 2003 so now we're into like  2005 and it's still not out and it's  still not finished and it's not even  being close to finished and I could  sense the impatience from my readers and  from my publishers and you know  rightfully so  so I and the book is so long you know  well what do I have to do what are my  choices well one I can make severe cuts  I can eliminate hundreds of pages what  I've written here and try to bring it to  a thing but I didn't like that thing I  liked what I'd written to I can split  the book into you know I can just sort  of end a book where it is I got 15 under  pages let me send it in and say here it  is published it and some characters  there'd be big stories some carriers of  the other stories but then it dawned on  me that I could split the book but I  could split the book geographically  split the book not not chronologically  but geographically by character taking  out and publishing the story lines that  were finished some of them required a  little more work I had to wrap them up  and complete and moving to Narus who  after all was on another con  and moving the stuff at the wall you  know - to the next book and that seemed  to me to be the best solution to this  day I don't know that it was necessarily  an ideal solution but I still think it  might have been the best solutions of  the of the options that I had given  given the circumstances that existed at  the time but it's hard to tell you know  