 do we  read fiction what what is it that  attracts us to reading a stories about  lands that that don't exist in people  who don't exist in conflicts that never  happened what's what's the the appeal of  fiction to my mind it's vicarious  experience I I think we live through  fiction you know I look back on the  books that I read when I was young  Lord of the Rings you know I remember  the Council of Elrond  I remember Sam and Frodo making their  way across the the plains of Moria I  remember these events almost as if they  happened to me I remember the fog and  the Barrow Downs these things have have  become part of my memory now I read  these when I was like 12 or 13 years old  I don't remember what I had for dinner  the night that I read about the Council  of Elrond I don't remember who sat  behind me in my junior high school class  or who's that in front of me or  I'd even have to think back to try to  figure out who the hell the teacher was  and I probably wouldn't be successful so  which of these is really part of my life  which isn't as I live not to get all  existential well I think it's the things  that we incorporate it's the things that  we remember we all of us as human beings  are sums of our memories and our lives  when we when we live our lives and we  reflect our lives vicarious experience  can be just as important as real  experience and that's why I've been  known to say that a reader lives a  thousand lives before he dies while the  man who does not read lives only one and  I think that's true you know as a reader  I've climbed mountains I've visited  other planets I've I've doped at a  bottom of the sea I've sailed the  Spanish mane and for pirates I loved a  thousand beautiful women  I've died I've had children I've had all  these experiences that I've never had in  real life but and yet these they're  they're part of me and they've made my  life immensely richer so that's what I  want to do for my reader I want when you  you're finished with my books I want you  to feel that you lived those books not  that they simply read some words on a  page so I want to immerse you in the  world and details are part of that  details are probably the most essential  part of that you know you you read some  of these modern writers and and they let  page turners the John Grisham's of the  world the James Patterson's of the world  there's nothing but dialogue they almost  read like screenplays you know two guys  talking in an office they never describe  the office they never describe what  they're doing you know a guy walks into  a bar you never see the bar I mean there  are bars and bars there are foreign bars  and their dive bars in there every bar  is different and and some bars are  unique I don't I don't want that kind of  fiction and I don't want to give my  readers that kind of fiction I want you  to see the place and smell the place and  taste the food and I want it to be as  real as if you were living a book and  that's why I I put so much detail in and  I recognize that this is not for  everybody this is  you know some people do not value that  in fiction some people and I've had  people tell me that they they skim over  all the details all that boring  descriptive part I don't want just get  on with the story you know well fine but  those people can read James wish them  you know I mean there's lots of readers  writers out there for those people who  don't want the immersive experience who  obviously get something else out of  their fiction then then what I want out  of fiction and what I'm trying to  provide to my readers so does that cover  it I hope it covers it like that with so  many complex characters how do you keep  that timeline straight so that works  with increasing difficulty the timelines  in dance with dragons drove me mad you  know trying to figure out I mean it's  it's it's not just a question of keeping  a calendar and all that there there are  dramatic demands to you know in terms of  the pacing of the book you want certain  certain events to come before other  events and you want to build a certain  climaxes and you don't want two things  stepping on each other's toes he was  there happening on different parts of  the world so I was always wrestling with  the winter what I called the Meereenese  knot when in in dance with dragons which  was the the order in which certain  people should arrive at marine and and  where events in marine should be when  this character arrived and where things  should have progressed to when this  character arrived and has this turn or  twist happened yet and rewriting it and  changing it and and I kept doing it  saying no this doesn't quite work  because now works for this character but  then this character sort of out of it  and it's sort of anti-climatic when he  shows up I got to get him there earlier  oh no but that that means this thing  doesn't work anymore so it's it's  challenging it's challenging so you know  I'm I'm a you know just kill a bunch of  these guys and that'll just simplify  things no end
 

